          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. For each number x in the list of divisors-of-m"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.134,
            0.881,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "- If x also appears in the list of divisors-of-n,add x to the list common-divisors"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.18,
            0.881,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "code_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.187,
                0.18,
                0.881,
                0.221
              ],
              "content": "5. Report the rightmost element of the list common-\ndivisors as gcd(m,n)"
            }
          ],
          "sub_type": "code",
          "guess_lang": "txt"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.235,
            0.883,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When we execute this algorithm for gcd(375, 825) we check whether each of the divisors [1, 3, 5, 15, 25, 75, 125, 375] of 375 appears in the list of divisors [1, 3, 5, 11, 15, 25, 33, 55, 75, 165, 275, 825] of 825. We see that 1, 3, 5, 15, 25 and 75 are in the second list while 125 and 375 are not. So we compute the list of common divisors to be [1, 3, 5, 15, 25, 75]. Since 75 is the rightmost element in this list, gcd(375, 825) = 75."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.376,
            0.421,
            0.399
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.3 Data Structures"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.409,
            0.882,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When we execute an algorithm, we compute intermediate quantities, such as the list divisors-of-m and the list divisors-of-n. To keep track of these values, we use names such as divisors-of-m and divisorsof-n, respectively, so that we can refer to them later in the algorithm. As we remarked earlier, assigning names to intermediate values is important for writing down algorithms concisely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.528,
            0.884,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Also, these intermediate values are typically more complicated than just simple numbers. In this case, we maintain lists of numbers and, in fact, lists that are arranged in ascending order. A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "list",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an example of a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "data structure",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Data structures allow us to organise information in a manner that helps make an algorithm more efficient. We will explore this idea in higher grades."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.365,
            0.677,
            0.631,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 11.2"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.725,
            0.882,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Suppose List 1 and List 2 are two lists of numbers in increasing order."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.768,
            0.881,
            0.81
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Write an algorithm to find elements in List 1 that are not present in List 2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.814,
            0.881,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Write an algorithm to find elements in List 2 that are not present in List 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.859,
            0.881,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Describe an algorithm to compute the least common multiple (lcm) of two numbers."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
